 more favorable (higher value) most probable outcome, it would prefer B (3000 > 1500). However, this depends on how 'most probable' is interpreted when probabilities are tied. Alternatively, if the reason selects the best outcome among those with maximum probability per lottery, then B's best such outcome is 3000, A's is 1500, so B is preferred. Thus, the reason would prefer Lottery B. But if the reason considers the certainty of the outcome, it might favor A—however, that would deviate from the strict definition. Based on the strict definition, the reason prefers the lottery whose most probable outcome (i.e., outcome with highest probability) is more favorable. Since B's highest-probability outcomes include 3000 (50%), and A's is 1500 (100%), and 3000 > 1500, the more favorable most probable outcome is in B. Therefore, the reason would prefer B. But note: this hinges on comparing values despite differences in probability. The reason does not weigh probability beyond identifying which outcome has the highest chance. So, the reason can be applied, and it would prefer B.",
